  "metadata": {
    "title": "Has The AI Power Boom Pushed Bloom Energy Stock Too Far, Too Fast?",
    "byline": null,
    "published_at": null,
    "site_name": "finance.yahoo.com",
    "language": null
  },
  "content": {
    "text": "The fuel-cell maker is benefiting from a significant AI-driven trend, but its long-term value will be determined by its performance in less favorable market conditions.\nIf you're watching the market's top performers, Bloom Energy (BE) is hard to miss. The stock has jumped 8.3% in the last five trading days, easily outpacing the S&P 500's 2.0% gain. The reason is a gusher of good news: on its latest earnings call, management announced a landmark deal to supply up to 2.45 gigawatts of power for Oracle's (ORCL) \"Project Jupiter\" AI factory and sharply raised its full-year revenue guidance.\nA run like that triggers a powerful instinct in any investor: the urge to chase a winner. You see a stock taking off, fueled by the insatiable power demands of artificial intelligence, and the fear of missing out can be overwhelming.\nBut building wealth isn't about catching every hot stock. The more important question is what owning Bloom Energy does to your entire portfolio. Are you adding a genuinely different source of returns, or are you just piling on a more volatile version of the same market risk you already have in your index funds? How it behaves over years, not days, is what truly matters.\nA Different Engine, Not Just A Faster One\nTo figure out if a stock is diversifying your risk, we look at its long-term relationship with the broader market. Bloom Energy's five-year correlation to the S&P 500 is 0.41. At 0.41, Bloom lives in a valuable middle ground.\nThis moderate correlation means that while the stock often shares the market's general direction, a substantial part of its performance is driven by its own story. And that story has been potent. Over the past five years, Bloom has delivered an annualized return of 58.6%, far outpacing the S&P 500's 13.9%. That combination is attractive: you get a high-return asset whose success is meaningfully independent of the market you already own. Rather than being a leveraged copy of the index, it provides a differentiated return stream.\nThe Price Of That Power Is Volatility\nThat unique performance profile comes with a kick. Bloom Energy tends to amplify the market's daily moves significantly. Over the past year, on days the S&P 500 went up, the stock captured about 551% of that gain. But on days the market fell, it absorbed about 344% of the loss.\nThis isn't a flaw; it's a crucial characteristic. The stock offers a powerful, distinct engine for growth, but it's not a smooth ride. This high volatility underscores that sizing your position is critical. It's an asset to be handled with discipline, not impulse.",
